<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm Excited! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I'm Happy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Excited!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From <span style="text-decoration: underline;">___</span> to <span style="text-decoration: underline;">___</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Recognize basic greetings and farewells.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize and respond to words related to emotions (e.g., 'happy,' 'sad,' or 'excited').
                Can follow instructions to express feelings, using gestures or facial expressions (e.g., Smiling in response to the prompt 'Show happiness.').
                Can participate in activities to identify or describe emotions when spoken (e.g., identifying emotions by pointing to the flashcard that represents the emotion when it is heard).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a cheerful tone, "Good morning, everyone! How are you today?" while using a 'happy' facial expression. The teacher then leads a simple "Hello Song" that includes actions for 'happy' and 'excited'. (e.g., "Hello, hello, how are you? I'm happy! I'm happy!"). Students listen and imitate gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards showing different emotions (happy, sad, excited, tired, angry). For each flashcard, the teacher clearly pronounces the word and models the corresponding facial expression and body gesture. For "happy" and "excited," the teacher exaggerates the /h/ sound in 'happy' and the /e/ sound in 'excited'. The teacher then says, "I'm happy!" and "I'm excited!" while pointing to themselves and the flashcard.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple questions and observing student responses:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I happy?" (Teacher shows a happy face, waits for head nod/shake or 'yes/no' sound from students).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me happy!" (Teacher prompts students to make a happy face or gesture, observing their actions).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short narrative story or dialogue using large, colorful picture cards or puppets. Genre: Narrative / Dialogue. Register: Informal. Format: Audio-visual (teacher reads/performs, students listen and observe).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "The Feeling Friends Story"</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces two puppet friends, "Leo" and "Mia." The teacher uses different voices for each puppet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher narrates a very short story: "Good morning, Leo! How are you today?" (Teacher uses Mia's voice). "I'm happy! I want to play," says Leo. (Teacher uses Leo's voice, making Leo happy). "Oh, look! Here comes Mia. How are you, Mia?" "I'm excited! I want to play with my friend!" (Mia's voice, excited). "But oh no, here is another friend, Sam. 'I'm sleepy,' says Sam. 'I need to rest.' " (Teacher uses Sam's sleepy voice and gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The story includes the target grammar: 'I'm happy,' 'How are you?', 'I'm excited,' 'I need to rest,' and 'I want to play.'</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing feelings and needs by saying, 'I'm happy.' or 'I'm tired.'; Responding to questions about feelings: Answering 'How are you?' with simple 'I'm...' statements (e.g., 'I'm excited.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening-focused):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "Who is happy in the story?" (Teacher points to the puppets, students listen and point to the correct puppet).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifying Emotions:</b> "Point to the friend who is sleepy." (Teacher asks, students listen and point). "Show me who is excited!" (Students listen and make an excited face).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Simple sentences ('I'm happy.'), WH-questions ('How are you?'), I'm + adjective ('I'm excited.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, sleepy, excited, angry, smile, frown.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /h/ in 'happy', /e/ in 'excited'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Recognizing the letter 'H' in 'happy' and 'E' in 'excited'.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher places large flashcards of various emotions (happy, sad, excited, tired, hungry, thirsty, sleepy) on the board or floor. The teacher says an emotion word clearly (e.g., "Happy!"), and students listen and point to the corresponding flashcard. The teacher repeats this with different emotion words. Teacher reinforces pronunciation of /h/ and /e/ for 'happy' and 'excited.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Act (TPR / Semi-controlled Practice):</b> The teacher says an emotion or a simple phrase like "Show me happy!" or "You are sleepy!" Students listen to the instruction and respond by making the appropriate facial expression and body gesture (e.g., smiling, yawning). The teacher can also say "Listen to the letter /h/ in happy, show me happy!" or "Listen to the letter /e/ in excited, show me excited!".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Musical Feelings (Listening & Peer Check Adaptation):</b> Students stand in a circle. The teacher plays short musical snippets or uses different vocal tones/sounds (e.g., a cheerful tune for 'happy', a slow lullaby for 'sleepy'). When the music/sound stops, the teacher calls out, "How does this make you feel?" and says an emotion word (e.g., "Sad!"). Students listen and then turn to a friend and show the 'sad' face. The teacher walks around, observing students listening and mimicking the emotions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Feeling Command Chain"</b> (Focus on listening and non-verbal/one-word response).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity Description:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by saying, "I'm happy!" and smiles, modeling the action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then whispers an emotion word (e.g., "Sad") into the ear of the student next to them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">That student listens to the whispered word, then silently acts out the emotion (e.g., makes a sad face) for the next student in the circle. They can also point to a flashcard if available.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The next student listens/observes their peer's action, then whispers the *same* emotion word they *think* it is, or acts it out to the next student. (The main focus here is listening to the whisper from the teacher, and then later, listening to the *visual cue* from the peer).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The chain continues. The last student in the chain, after receiving the action/whisper, says the emotion out loud, "I'm [emotion]!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The class then compares if the last emotion said matches the initial emotion whispered by the teacher. This emphasizes careful listening and observation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This task directly connects to the Theme "I'm Excited!" and Scenario "I'm Happy!" by requiring students to recognize and express basic emotions based on verbal and non-verbal cues.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen, Point, and Show"</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher will individually call out an emotion word or a simple question about feelings (e.g., "Point to 'hungry'," "Show me 'excited'," "How are you?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the prompt and then either point to the correct flashcard (placed on their desk or a common table) or make the appropriate facial expression/gesture.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Recognition:</b> Student accurately points to the correct emotion flashcard when heard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Response to Instruction:</b> Student follows instructions to express feelings using appropriate gestures or facial expressions (e.g., smiling in response to 'Show happiness').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Participation:</b> Student attempts to engage in the listening activities.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me (thumbs up or down) if you can find the 'happy' picture when I say 'happy'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me (thumbs up or down) if you can show a 'sad' face."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new feeling words did you hear today?" (Encourage pointing to flashcards or making gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which feeling is your favorite to show?" (Students gesture or point).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., TPR activities for showing emotions, use of puppets for engagement, clear pronunciation modeling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., More repetition for 'hungry' and 'thirsty' vocabulary, slower pace for some activities, ensuring all students get individual turns to listen and respond). Perhaps integrate a short song for each new feeling to help retention.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a simple illustrated text that uses these same emotion words. This listening foundation will help them recognize the words visually in print and understand the context of the story.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>